-- title:
--   In characteristic ℓ, j(qᵖ)notin K(j(q)) for p≠ℓ
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field of characteristic $\ell$, with $\ell$ prime, and let $p$ be a prime with $p \ne \ell$. Work inside the field `LaurentSeries K` of formal Laurent series over $K$, viewed as a $K$-algebra. Here `jqModC K` is the formal $q$-expansion of the modular invariant with its integral coefficients pushed into $K$: it is the product of the Hahn series $q^{-1}$ (the monomial `HahnSeries.single (-1) 1`) with the image in `LaurentSeries K` of the integral power series `jNum` $=$ `eisenstein4 ^ 3 * dedekindEtaUnitInv` under the coefficient map $\mathbb{Z} \to K$. For $N \neq 0$, `jqNModC K N` is the image of `jqModC K` under the ring homomorphism `qExpand K N`, which multiplies all Hahn-series exponents by $N$, i.e. performs the substitution $q \mapsto q^{N}$; thus `jqNModC K p` is $j(q^{p})$. The assertion is that $j(q^{p})$ does not belong to `IntermediateField.adjoin K {jqModC K}`, the intermediate field of `LaurentSeries K` over $K$ generated by $j(q)$; in classical notation, $j(q^{p}) \notin K\bigl(j(q)\bigr)$.
--
--   This is the characteristic-$\ell$ separability statement for the degeneracy map $q \mapsto q^{p}$ on $q$-expansions: the hypothesis $p \ne \ell$ cannot be dropped, since in characteristic $\ell$ one has $j(q^{\ell}) = j(q)^{\ell}$, which lies in $K(j(q))$. It underlies the study of the function field of $X_0(N)$ over $K$ in this formalisation, being used for the irreducibility of the reduced modular polynomial over $K(j)$, for the count of poles of $j(q)$ in the full modular function field, and for the construction of automorphisms of the field of $q$-expansions fixing $j(q)$.
